{
  "id": "Compound::DB00381 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Amlodipine, a calcium channel blocker, is linked to Hypertension (disease) through a series of molecular and pharmacological relationships. The evidence suggests that Amlodipine, as part of the dihydropyridine class, is included in both dihydropyridines and calcium channel antagonists. This classification places Amlodipine within a broader family of drugs that act on calcium channels, which are key targets in the treatment of hypertension. The pathway also highlights that Isradipine, a dihydropyridine, is used to treat hypertension, reinforcing the connection between the drug class and the disease. The shared ancestry of Amlodipine and Isradipine, through the Methyl Ester and Calcium Channel Blocker categories, further supports the notion that Amlodipine exerts its therapeutic effect via a mechanism that is broadly applicable to calcium channel-targeting drugs. The integration of these relationships underscores the role of Amlodipine in the management of hypertension through its pharmacological action on calcium channels.",
